tool_args.old_string:
\begin{abstract}
Multi-judge panels are widely deployed for robust Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation, yet adding judges can shift, not reduce, the dominant vulnerability pathway. Existing defenses conflate panel diversity with individual judge robustness, and it remains unclear which factor drives attack success rate (ASR) reduction. We decompose panel ASR into weakest-link susceptibility ($\eta_{\max}$) and effective independent channels ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) across 15 models, six attack types, two multiple-choice benchmarks, and panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (up to 6,435 panels). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient is positive in all 12 conditions under ordinary least squares, but significance varies across inference methods (0 to 12 of 12), and the diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$) limits causal claims. The dominant predictor shifts with panel size: at $K{=}3$, both $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ contribute; at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominates while $\eta_{\max}$ attenuates. Aggregation rules modulate predictability ($R^2$: threshold 0.83, Bayesian 0.09). Small panels benefit most from individual robustness screening; larger panels require attention to judge independence.
\end{abstract}

tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
Multi-judge panels are widely used to defend Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation against manipulation, yet no existing work disentangles whether robustness gains stem from individual judge quality or inter-judge diversity. We decompose panel attack success rate into weakest-link susceptibility ($\eta_{\max}$) and effective independence ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) across 15 models, six attack types, two multiple-choice benchmarks, and panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (up to 6,435 panels). Two findings are robust across diversity metrics and inference methods: (1)~diversity is uniformly positively associated with vulnerability (both $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\kappa$-diversity confirm positive direction in 12/12 conditions), and (2)~$\eta_{\max}$'s predictive power diminishes monotonically with panel size (meta-analytic pooled $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$: $0.264 \to 0.101 \to 0.084$; confidence interval crosses zero at $K{=}7$). Monte Carlo calibration confirms the wild cluster bootstrap is conservative (max null rejection $1.6\%$ at $\alpha{=}5\%$). Aggregation rules modulate predictability ($R^2$: threshold 0.83, Bayesian 0.09). The diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$) precludes causal claims; all findings are associational.
\end{abstract}

